🐛 Describe the bug
When applying Chebyshev or Shifted Chebyshev polynomials from torch.special to NaN inputs on CPU, the operations sometimes return uninitialized memory (often manifesting as arbitrary finite values from adjacent element computations) instead of correctly propagating NaN. This issue is particularly easily exposed when the input tensor is non-contiguous.
This leads to undefined behavior and silent data corruption.Depending on the memory state, the operation can manifest in highly unpredictable ways: in some cases, it mistakenly copies arbitrary finite values from adjacent element computations (e.g., -4.0 or -1.0); in other cases, it returns completely extreme garbage values(e.g., 1.83e+31 or 8.31e-39).
This currently affects the following operations on CPU:

torch.special.chebyshev_polynomial_u
torch.special.shifted_chebyshev_polynomial_t
torch.special.shifted_chebyshev_polynomial_u
torch.special.shifted_chebyshev_polynomial_w

To Reproduce
import torch

# Non-contiguous tensor with shape=(2, 3), stride=(1, 2)
vals = torch.tensor(
    [
        [float("-inf"), float("nan"), float("inf")],
        [-0.0,          0.0,          1.0],
    ],
    device="cpu",
    dtype=torch.float32,
)
x = torch.empty((3, 2), device="cpu", dtype=torch.float32).t()
x.copy_(vals)

# Example: shifted_chebyshev_polynomial_u with n=3
y_noncontig = torch.special.shifted_chebyshev_polynomial_u(x, 3)
y_contig = torch.special.shifted_chebyshev_polynomial_u(x.contiguous(), 3)

print("x[0, 1]               =", x[0, 1].item())
print("noncontiguous y[0, 1] =", y_noncontig[0, 1].item())
print("contiguous y[0, 1]    =", y_contig[0, 1].item())

torch.testing.assert_close(y_noncontig, y_contig, equal_nan=True, rtol=0, atol=0)
Actual output:
x[0, 1]               = nan
noncontiguous y[0, 1] = -4.0
contiguous y[0, 1]    = nan
AssertionError: Tensor-likes are not equal!

Expected behavior
The operation should correctly propagate NaN regardless of the memory layout (stride) of the input tensor. Both contiguous and non-contiguous inputs should result in NaN for y[0, 1].
Root Cause Analysis
The issue originates from the C++ scalar implementations in aten/src/ATen/native/Math.h. Taking shifted_chebyshev_polynomial_u_forward as an example:



      pytorch/aten/src/ATen/native/Math.h


        Lines 3750 to 3795
      in
      a5d44b2







           template<typename T> 




           inline C10_HOST_DEVICE T shifted_chebyshev_polynomial_u_forward(T x, int64_t n) { 




               if (n < 0) { 




                   return T(0.0); 




               } 









               if (x == T(1.0)) { 




                   return n + 1; 




               } 









               if (x == T(0.0)) { 




                   if (n % 2 == 0) { 




                       return n + 1; 




                   } 









                   return -(n + 1); 




               } 









               if ((n > 6) && (std::abs(x + x - T(1.0)) < T(1.0))) { 




                   if (std::sin(std::acos(x + x - T(1.0))) != T(0.0)) { 




                       return std::sin((n + 1) * std::acos(x + x - T(1.0))) / std::sin(std::acos(x + x - T(1.0))); 




                   } 









                   return (n + 1) * std::cos((n + 1) * std::acos(x + x - T(1.0))) / (x + x - T(1.0)); 




               } 









               if (n == 0) { 




                   return T(1.0); 




               } 









               if (n == 1) { 




                   return x + x - T(1.0) + (x + x - T(1.0)); 




               } 









               T p = T(1.0); 




               T q = x + x - T(1.0) + (x + x - T(1.0)); 




               T r; 









               for (int64_t k = 2; (k <= n) && !std::isnan(q); k++) { 




                   r = (x + x - T(1.0) + (x + x - T(1.0))) * q - p; 




                   p = q; 




                   q = r; 




               } 









               return r; 




           } // shifted_chebyshev_polynomial_u_forward(T x, int64_t n) 





    T p = T(1.0);
    T q = x + x - T(1.0) + (x + x - T(1.0));
    T r; // <--- Uninitialized

    for (int64_t k = 2; (k <= n) && !std::isnan(q); k++) {
        r = (x + x - T(1.0) + (x + x - T(1.0))) * q - p;
        p = q;
        q = r;
    }

    return r; // <--- Returned uninitialized if loop is skipped
When x = NaN and n >= 2, q becomes NaN immediately. As a result, the loop condition !std::isnan(q) evaluates to false, causing the loop to be skipped entirely. The function then returns r, which was never initialized.
Under the hood, TensorIterator's vectorized paths for non-contiguous memory layouts make this uninitialized value highly visible—it simply picks up whatever value was left in the register or stack from calculating the previous contiguous element (e.g., -4.0 leftover from computing x = 0.0).
This exact pattern exists in multiple forward functions inside Math.h:

chebyshev_polynomial_u_forward
shifted_chebyshev_polynomial_t_forward
shifted_chebyshev_polynomial_u_forward
shifted_chebyshev_polynomial_v_forward
shifted_chebyshev_polynomial_w_forward

Versions
torch: 2.14.0.dev20260618+cu130
CUDA used to build PyTorch: 13.0
OS: Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS (x86_64)
Is CUDA available: True
CUDA runtime version: 12.0.140
